  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea **is to transform the discrete optimization problem** into a continuous domain optimization problem with dimension reduced by Factor Analysis. This approach leverages the fact that highly oscillatory candidates are dominant in minimization, and represents functions using a sparse set of active components. These components are updated in the Fourier domain, preserving orthogonality and harmonic cancellations. The method solves:\n\n```\n||F(h) - f_targets||^2_2\n```\nsubject to:\n```\n0 <= h_i <= 1, sum(h_i) = n/2\n```\n\nwhere F(h) is a Fourier transform-based projection of the step function.\n\nThis differs from current methods by:\n- Reducing dimensionality from n to O(n^(1/2))\n- Enforcing mathematical exactness through spectral decomposition\n- Adapting to solution structure through periodic active-set updates\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nThe representation uses Gaussian components in the Fourier domain:\n\nLet \u03c9_k = 2\u03c0k/n, k=0,1,...,(n/2)\n\nDefine the acceleration function:\n```\ns(k) = |sin(\u03c9_k / 2)|^2\n```\n\nThe optimization vector satisfies:\n```\nh_i = \u220f_{j=1}^m (1 + a_j sin(2\u03c0k_j(i+1/2)))\n```\n\nThe initial active set is determined by:\n```\n\u03b1_m = sort(-logistic(beta * \u03ba_m)), m=0,...,n/4\n```\n\nWhere \u03ba are auto-correlation scores.\n\nThe matrix conditions become:\n```\nA^T A = I, s.t. diag(weights) = W_{k}= s(k)/||\u03c8_k||^2\n```\n\nAnd the projection becomes:\n```\nh(tau) = \u03a3 \u03c4 * exp(-(tau - psi)) / sum(\u03c4)\n```\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n1. Validation and initialization\n\n   a) Input resolution (parent: use `initial_h_values` at n=160 unless specified)\n     \n   b) Define active basis functions from Fourier modes:\n      ```\n      psi_k(tau) = sin(2\u03c0k*t*tau/n), k=0,1,...,floor(n/4)\n      ```\n   \n   c) Compute spectral projections with:\n      ```\n      w_i = exp(- (h[i] - 0.5)**2 / 0.01)\n      ```\n   \n2. Coordinate transformation\n   \n   a) Transform to u-coordinates (Fourier space):\n     \n      ```\n      u = np.fft.rfft(initial_h_values)\n      U_min = np.real(u) - 0.3\n      U_max = np.real(u) + 0.3\n      ```\n   \n   b) Active set selection (by correlation score):\n     ```\n     act_idx = np.argsort(-|u|)[0:5]\n     ```\n   \n3. Iterative decomposition:\n   \n   a) Spectral gradient ascent (500 steps):\n     ```\n     h_new += 0.001 * np.gradient(u)\n     h_new = project_h(h_new, n)\n     ```\n   \n   b) Auto-correlation projection:\n     ```\n     h = optimal_barycentric(projections, penalty)\n     ```\n\n4. Feasibility restoration\n\n   Use softmax with volume constraint:\n     ```\n     h = exp(grid_value) / sum(exp(grid_value))\n\n   subject to:\n     sum(h) == n/2\n   ```\n\nWith Mosek for semi-definite projection\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Default resolution: n=160\n- Active set: m=5 components\n- Iterations: 500\n- learning rate: 0.1\n- regularization: 0.01\n\nMultiple variations:\n\n1. Low-frequency version (less variations)\n2. Hybrid optimization with BFGS\n3. Multi-scale integration (with annealing)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Dominant cost: FFT and matrix inversion at 100Hz interval\n- Fallback: when variance > 1e-5, fall back to guarded probabilistic projection\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nGenerate n-long vectors satisfying:\n```\n0<=h_i<=1, sum(h_i)=n/2\n```\n\nCheck output with:\n```\nassert all(0<=x<1 for x in h_vec)\nassert abs(sum(h_vec) - n/2) < 1e-5\n```\n\nRequires scipy.fft and cvxpy with Mosek.",
